WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.263 --> 00:00:12.180
SHAILESH SHUKLA: Good
morning, good afternoon,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.100
and good evening, everyone.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:16.910
Thank you for joining us,
and welcome to What's New

00:00:16.910 --> 00:00:19.040
and What's Next for Networking.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:21.860
I'm Shailesh Shukla, Vice
President and General Manager

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:23.267
for Networking.

00:00:23.267 --> 00:00:25.100
WENDY CARTEE: And I'm
Wendy Cartee, Director

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.930
of Outbound Product Management.

00:00:26.930 --> 00:00:28.760
We had a very busy
year, and we're

00:00:28.760 --> 00:00:31.950
so excited to share what
we've been working on.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.830
SHAILESH SHUKLA: We
have a packed session,

00:00:33.830 --> 00:00:37.460
and we'll cover our Cloud
networking portfolio, what's

00:00:37.460 --> 00:00:41.510
new in networking, network
security, and monitoring.

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:44.870
But first, the
why of networking.

00:00:44.870 --> 00:00:48.170
In addition to being a critical
part of the global Cloud

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:53.120
Infrastructure, networking also
drives real business outcomes

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.770
for our customers.

00:00:54.770 --> 00:00:58.850
As workloads and workforces
become increasingly global

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.850
and increasingly
distributed, the network

00:01:01.850 --> 00:01:05.810
is at the front and center
of connecting, securing,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:08.210
and enabling everything,
while delivering

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.960
a seamless experience to
all applications, all users,

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:13.850
and all devices.

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:16.160
For example, our
retail customers

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.460
tell us that a high-performance
network is directly

00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.300
tied to superior user
experience, which translates

00:01:23.300 --> 00:01:25.050
to faster revenue conversion.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:26.570
That's a top line.

00:01:26.570 --> 00:01:29.520
I don't want to go through
each of these items here,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.030
but what you will
see is our customers

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:35.210
tell us, in all verticals,
across the world,

00:01:35.210 --> 00:01:40.070
that networking is a key enabler
for their business outcomes.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.730
Our Cloud Networking vision
is centered on our customers,

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.100
and the vision is this--

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:51.000
planet-scale networking for
a smart and connected world.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.640
Google has built a massive
planet-scale network

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:57.480
infrastructure serving
billions of users today.

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:01.110
And we are leveraging
advanced AI, automation,

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.740
and programmability to build
a self-driving network that

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:09.930
makes it simple, smart, trusted,
and open, so our customers can

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:14.280
focus on running and
enabling their business.

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.490
Our global network
is at your service,

00:02:16.490 --> 00:02:23.090
with 27 regions, 82 zones,
146 edge PoPs, thousands

00:02:23.090 --> 00:02:27.110
of edge nodes, 16 subsea
cable investments,

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:29.900
connecting more than 200
countries and territories

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.500
worldwide.

00:02:31.500 --> 00:02:33.600
We are proud to serve
billions of users,

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.620
while staying 100% carbon
neutral since 2007.

00:02:37.620 --> 00:02:39.510
And we continue
to invest heavily

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:41.490
in our infrastructure,
our network.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.740
And just this year,
we have already

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:45.930
added four new Cloud regions--

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:49.950
Warsaw, Delhi,
Melbourne, and Toronto.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:53.130
In addition, we have
announced five subsea cables--

00:02:53.130 --> 00:02:58.590
Echo, Firmina, Blue,
Raman, and Apricot,

00:02:58.590 --> 00:03:02.230
which connect all parts of
the world from Asia, Europe,

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:05.280
Africa, Middle East,
to the Americas.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.160
On top of this massive
global network,

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:12.540
we offer a broad portfolio of
network and security services

00:03:12.540 --> 00:03:16.320
that connect, secure,
scale, optimize,

00:03:16.320 --> 00:03:19.890
and modernize your network
and your infrastructure.

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:21.990
To do that, we have
this full stack

00:03:21.990 --> 00:03:23.940
of networking
services from layer 1

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.150
to layer 7, that provide
high-performance networking

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.700
for all your workloads
running in VMs,

00:03:29.700 --> 00:03:32.490
in containers, or on Bare Metal.

00:03:32.490 --> 00:03:36.280
2021 has been an incredibly
exciting year for us.

00:03:36.280 --> 00:03:38.590
We added several new
products this year,

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:42.630
including the Google Distributed
Cloud, Cloud IDS Service,

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.690
and enhanced our service
networking portfolio,

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:48.630
with Network Connectivity
Center, Private Service

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:52.650
Connect, Network Intelligence
Center enhancements, et cetera.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:54.730
Wendy will tell you
a lot more about it.

00:03:54.730 --> 00:03:55.620
Let's get started.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:56.412
Wendy, over to you.

00:03:56.412 --> 00:03:57.412
WENDY CARTEE: All right.

00:03:57.412 --> 00:03:58.470
Thank you, Shailesh.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:00.610
We have a lot of
exciting announcements,

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.360
so let's take a look at
what's new in networking.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.030
I would like to introduce
our Google Distributed Cloud

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:10.080
strategy, which provides a fully
managed, integrated hardware

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.990
and software solution that
extends Google Cloud's

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:16.860
infrastructure and services to
edge locations and customers'

00:04:16.860 --> 00:04:18.329
data center.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:22.019
Our Distributed Cloud
solution is enabled by Anthos

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:24.550
through a centralized
control plane,

00:04:24.550 --> 00:04:27.840
so customers can build and
run modern applications

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:32.260
anywhere with low-latency
and operational efficiency.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.270
Google Distributed
Cloud is ideal

00:04:34.270 --> 00:04:37.570
for local data processing,
low-latency edge

00:04:37.570 --> 00:04:40.990
compute workloads, modernizing
on-prem environments,

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:44.440
running sensitive workloads to
meet sovereignty requirements,

00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:48.790
and offering private 5G LTE
solutions for enterprise

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:50.230
customers.

00:04:50.230 --> 00:04:54.310
It can be deployed at Google
Edge, operator edge, customer

00:04:54.310 --> 00:04:57.940
edge, and/or customer
data centers.

00:04:57.940 --> 00:05:01.090
We announced Network
Connectivity Center, or NCC,

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:05.050
a few months ago, and it
is now generally available.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:08.800
NCC provides a simple and
consistent management model

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:11.170
to connect your on-prem
and Cloud networking

00:05:11.170 --> 00:05:12.940
needs seamlessly.

00:05:12.940 --> 00:05:16.870
With NCC, you can connect
VPNs, partner and dedicated

00:05:16.870 --> 00:05:20.950
interconnects, SD-WANs, and
third-party router appliances

00:05:20.950 --> 00:05:22.570
in a hub-and-spoke model.

00:05:22.570 --> 00:05:25.060
And we've partnered
with SD-WAN leaders

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:29.860
like Cisco, Fortinet, Palo Alto
Networks, Versa, and VMware,

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:32.650
enabling you to
simplify connectivity

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:35.250
from branch to Cloud.

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:39.390
In addition to
network-layer connectivity,

00:05:39.390 --> 00:05:42.420
we also offer
service-layer connectivity,

00:05:42.420 --> 00:05:46.240
with Private Service
Connect, or PSC.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:49.930
PSC provides a service-oriented
networking model

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:53.470
by abstracting the underlying
network, such as pairing,

00:05:53.470 --> 00:05:56.290
IP address,
configuration, et cetera.

00:05:56.290 --> 00:06:00.280
PSC allows you to create
private and secure connections

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:03.250
from your Cloud networks
to producer services,

00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:05.980
such as Cloud Storage,
Cloud Bigtable,

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:11.780
or third-party services from
Elastic, MongoDB, or Snowflake.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.250
PSC is now GA in all
regions, and we introduced

00:06:16.250 --> 00:06:19.290
three new enhancements.

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:23.220
PSC with Consumer Service
Controls is now in preview.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:27.060
With HTTPS Service
Controls, PSC allows

00:06:27.060 --> 00:06:31.110
customers to have finer grained
control of their own policies,

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:35.040
and support data regionalization
for sovereign clouds.

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:39.660
We added HTTPS internal load
balancer as a PSC target

00:06:39.660 --> 00:06:41.730
to help scale services.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.940
For example, you can
configure the load balancer

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.520
to log all requests
to Cloud logging,

00:06:47.520 --> 00:06:52.660
or choose which services are
available using a URL map.

00:06:52.660 --> 00:06:55.570
PSC is integrated
with Service Directory

00:06:55.570 --> 00:06:59.860
so you can auto register
available services to make

00:06:59.860 --> 00:07:04.210
service consumption
even faster and simpler.

00:07:04.210 --> 00:07:09.460
This is so cool that companies
like Bloomberg, Elastic Cloud,

00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:14.110
and MongoDB shared why they are
using Private Service Connect.

00:07:14.110 --> 00:07:17.110
And we hope you will
also test drive PSC

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:19.730
and let us know how you like it.

00:07:19.730 --> 00:07:21.440
Now, moving up
the service stack,

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:24.910
let's talk about GKE
and Anthos networking.

00:07:24.910 --> 00:07:27.550
We released a lot
of features in GKE

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.490
and Anthos networking
recently, and we are only

00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:32.490
going to cover four today.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:37.450
First, GKE gateway controller
is Google Cloud's implementation

00:07:37.450 --> 00:07:40.480
of the Kubernetes
Gateway API, and it

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:44.110
manages internal
and external HTTPS,

00:07:44.110 --> 00:07:49.780
load-balancing for a GKE cluster
or a fleet of GKE clusters.

00:07:49.780 --> 00:07:53.440
It is currently in preview
and will GA this quarter.

00:07:53.440 --> 00:07:57.760
Second, Cloud DNS for GKE
supports the full Kubernetes

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:01.930
DNS specification, and
it will GA this quarter.

00:08:01.930 --> 00:08:05.020
It provides pod and
service-level resolution,

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:08.470
with a fully managed DNS
that does not require

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:10.790
a cluster-hosted DNS provider.

00:08:10.790 --> 00:08:16.140
So it removes the overhead of
managing cluster-hosted DNS.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:20.100
For Bare Metal fans out there,
a BGP-based load balancer

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:23.070
for Anthos Bare Metal
is now in preview,

00:08:23.070 --> 00:08:25.650
supporting N-way
active-active redundancy

00:08:25.650 --> 00:08:29.190
for faster failover and
efficient utilization

00:08:29.190 --> 00:08:31.130
of bandwidth.

00:08:31.130 --> 00:08:32.990
And lastly, with
the increasing use

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.330
of container network
functions for CNFs,

00:08:35.330 --> 00:08:38.000
we are introducing
multi-NIC pods

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.470
on Anthos Bare
Metal, which allows

00:08:40.470 --> 00:08:44.090
pods to attach to multiple
network interfaces for use

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.330
cases such as data and
control planes operations,

00:08:47.330 --> 00:08:51.540
multi-tenancy, management
functions, et cetera.

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.600
Now, we covered edge,
hybrid connectivity,

00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:58.350
service-layer networking,
GKE, Anthos networking.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:08:59.640
What's next?

00:08:59.640 --> 00:09:03.220
We're going to talk about
Application Delivery.

00:09:03.220 --> 00:09:05.140
Our Application
Delivery portfolio

00:09:05.140 --> 00:09:09.070
offers layer-7 load balancing
and layer-4 network load

00:09:09.070 --> 00:09:10.120
balancing.

00:09:10.120 --> 00:09:12.490
In the layer-7 load
balancing family,

00:09:12.490 --> 00:09:15.670
we are introducing regional
external load balancer,

00:09:15.670 --> 00:09:18.730
which supports
distribution of HTTP

00:09:18.730 --> 00:09:23.200
and HTTPS traffic to backends
hosted on Compute Engine

00:09:23.200 --> 00:09:25.320
and GKE.

00:09:25.320 --> 00:09:27.570
Our layer-7 load
balancers are now

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:31.680
converged on Envoy, a
CNCF open-source project,

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:34.320
as the common data plane.

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:37.980
Leveraging Envoy Proxy, the
regional external load balancer

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.770
provides advanced traffic
management capabilities

00:09:40.770 --> 00:09:43.270
such as routing and
traffic policies,

00:09:43.270 --> 00:09:46.900
so you can steer traffic
where you want it.

00:09:46.900 --> 00:09:51.890
In addition, we added support
for hybrid load balancing.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.850
Hybrid load balancing supports
workloads running on-prem

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:58.070
in Google Cloud
or in other clouds

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:01.160
to help customers with
migration, performance

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:04.220
optimization, and
high availability.

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.730
You can use external
or internal HTTPS load

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:11.870
balancing, in addition
to TCP SSL proxy.

00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:15.140
Together, they're not only
load-balance endpoints

00:10:15.140 --> 00:10:18.230
on GCP, on-prem,
or other clouds,

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:20.660
they can extend Cloud
Armor to provide

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:23.000
DDoS protection
to your services,

00:10:23.000 --> 00:10:27.450
and leverage Cloud CDN for
low-latency performance.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:32.060
Next, let's take a quick
look at DNS enhancements.

00:10:32.060 --> 00:10:35.270
Now today, thousands of
customers use our Cloud DNS.

00:10:35.270 --> 00:10:38.300
And we've added a
lot of enhancements.

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:41.510
One to highlight is
Cloud DNS Policy Manager,

00:10:41.510 --> 00:10:43.370
which is in preview.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.760
It enables you to steer
private and internet traffic

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:50.440
using dynamic responses
from Cloud DNS.

00:10:50.440 --> 00:10:53.710
Policies may be set up
using weighted round robin

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.380
and/or geolocation
rules, and includes

00:10:56.380 --> 00:11:00.570
health checks, which can be
used for automatic failovers.

00:11:00.570 --> 00:11:02.550
You can use this
to manage traffic

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:05.580
in a multi-vendor
environment, direct traffic,

00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:09.140
and set up failover
traffic patterns.

00:11:09.140 --> 00:11:13.310
Let's take a look at what's
new in network security.

00:11:13.310 --> 00:11:17.210
We announced many new security
enhancements this year,

00:11:17.210 --> 00:11:21.080
such as hierarchical firewall,
Cloud Armor rate limiting,

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:22.970
and Cloud IDS.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:26.600
We are excited to announce
the preview of Cloud Armor

00:11:26.600 --> 00:11:30.980
bot and fraud management, which
leverages reCAPTCHA Enterprise

00:11:30.980 --> 00:11:34.070
to detect and stop
unwanted bot activity

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:36.230
at the edge of the network.

00:11:36.230 --> 00:11:39.740
This integrated DDoS, WAF,
and bot detection solution

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:42.500
uses signals collected
in the browser,

00:11:42.500 --> 00:11:47.420
plus threat data to automate
the detection of fraud.

00:11:47.420 --> 00:11:50.390
While customers can use these
products together already,

00:11:50.390 --> 00:11:52.910
we are simplifying
the deployment process

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:55.920
and providing a more
integrated experience

00:11:55.920 --> 00:11:59.990
so customers can accelerate
their time to value.

00:11:59.990 --> 00:12:02.330
For more information,
please attend the session

00:12:02.330 --> 00:12:08.270
on DDoS, WAF, firewalls, and
network-based threat detection.

00:12:08.270 --> 00:12:10.480
Next, we announced
Cloud IDS in July

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:13.090
with our partner
Palo Alto Networks.

00:12:13.090 --> 00:12:16.450
Cloud IDS provides a fully
managed network threat

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:19.510
detection service, with
high levels of security

00:12:19.510 --> 00:12:21.610
efficacy and low noise.

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:24.220
We worked closely with
Palo Alto Networks

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:28.480
to embed their industry leading
threat detection technologies,

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.710
and deeply integrated them
into our Cloud, beyond having

00:12:32.710 --> 00:12:37.880
a uniform usage model across
the UI, APIs, and command line.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:41.590
This means that customers can
pick from GCP native targets,

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:45.310
including anything from a
VPC to a specific workload,

00:12:45.310 --> 00:12:47.440
and even leverage
our flexible tagging

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:51.580
capabilities to identify
what they want to monitor.

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:56.290
We surface the alert information
in the Google Cloud UI, APIs,

00:12:56.290 --> 00:12:59.170
and Cloud Logging systems,
plus feed the data

00:12:59.170 --> 00:13:03.310
to our Security Command Center,
Chronicle, and third-party SIEM

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:04.930
and SOAR products.

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:08.680
It's quick and easy to deploy,
as it is managed, scaled,

00:13:08.680 --> 00:13:11.860
and operated by Google,
with high performance

00:13:11.860 --> 00:13:15.100
and availability all built in.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:16.840
Now, with all the
great products,

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.620
what about day-2 apps?

00:13:18.620 --> 00:13:22.230
Let's take a look at monitoring.

00:13:22.230 --> 00:13:25.350
Our flagship monitoring product,
Network intelligence Center,

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:29.160
has been expanded with
several new modules recently.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.270
Network Topology lets you view
actual flows, traffic pattern

00:13:33.270 --> 00:13:37.560
changes, ingress-egress bytes
over VPN and Interconnect

00:13:37.560 --> 00:13:42.390
to and from the internet
and GCP managed services.

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:46.380
Support for VPNs and
Interconnect is now in preview,

00:13:46.380 --> 00:13:51.440
and support for Google
managed services is now GA.

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.630
Global Performance
Dashboard gives you

00:13:53.630 --> 00:13:56.780
real-time metrics, such
as latency and packet

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:59.180
loss for the Google
Cloud network,

00:13:59.180 --> 00:14:02.930
as well as visibility into the
performance of your projects'

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:04.310
resources.

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:06.680
With connectivity
tests, you can check

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:09.770
the connectivity
between a VM instance

00:14:09.770 --> 00:14:14.510
and GCP-managed services,
such as GKE and Cloud SQL,

00:14:14.510 --> 00:14:17.640
and troubleshoot
issues at any time.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:19.400
Well, I hope you
enjoyed the What's

00:14:19.400 --> 00:14:21.450
New in Networking session.

00:14:21.450 --> 00:14:23.480
To learn more,
please take the time

00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:26.510
to view the deeper dive
sessions on these topics,

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:29.490
and visit our website
for more information.

00:14:29.490 --> 00:14:31.960
Thank you for joining us.

